      {
        "id": "comment-title",
        "type": "comment",
        "position": {
          "x": -360.0,
          "y": -180.0
        },
        "data": {
          "text": "# Staggered singular-value learning in deep linear networks\n\nSaxe, McClelland & Ganguli (2013) show that gradient descent on a deep linear network learns the modes of the input-output cross-covariance **one at a time**, from the largest singular value down.\n\n**Key claims to test:**\n- W_eff = W_3 W_2 W_1 starts near zero (small init).\n- σ₁(W_eff) rises first, then σ₂, then σ₃ — each after the previous saturates.\n- The time to learn mode k scales roughly as 1/σ_k(W*).\n- Effective rank of W_eff grows in discrete steps.\n\n**Setup:** 3-layer deep linear network (identity activations) trained with SGD on a noiseless linear dataset (8 → 8)."
        },
        "parentId": null,
        "extent": null,
        "hidden": null,
        "style": null
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-notes",
        "type": "comment",
        "position": {
          "x": -360.0,
          "y": 160.0
        },
        "data": {
          "text": "**Notes:**\n- `activation = identity` makes `mlp_model` a deep linear network.\n- `noiseLevel = 0` removes additive Gaussian noise so the oracle W* is exactly learned.\n- `depth = 2` gives three weight matrices (W₁, W₂, W₃).\n- `saxe_initialization` (ε = 0.01) sets each Wᵢ = 0.01·Q (Q orthogonal). This is the key condition for the theory: W_eff(0) ≈ 0 so modes are learned sequentially.\n- `learningRate = 0.005` is in the gradient-flow (small-step) regime.\n- The observable tracks **σ₁ … σ₄** of W₃ W₂ W₁ at each log step.\n- Watch the curves rise sequentially in the viz below the trainer."
        },
        "parentId": null,
        "extent": null,
        "hidden": null,
        "style": null
      },
